{
  // Type-checks backend/scripts/, which `tsconfig.build.json` does NOT: that project inherits
  // `"include": ["src/**/*"]` and `"rootDir": "./src"` from tsconfig.json, so adding scripts/
  // there would put files outside rootDir and change the emitted dist/ layout that `npm start`
  // depends on. A separate noEmit project checks them without touching what gets built.
  //
  // `npm run build` runs this, so CI covers it. (It was once CI's ONLY backend typecheck; the
  // backend job now also runs `npm run typecheck` -- tsconfig.json, which covers all of src/
  // including the suites and the seeds. This project is still the only thing checking scripts/.)
  //
  // A RATCHET, NOT A WISHLIST. The include is the whole tree and the exclude is an explicit
  // debt list, so a NEW script is covered the moment it is added and the list can only shrink.
  // The inverse (an allowlist of the few clean files) would have left every future script
  // silently unchecked until somebody remembered to add it.
  //
  // scripts/ had never been type-checked before this file existed, so the excluded entries
  // below carry 22 pre-existing errors. Delete an entry once its file is clean; do not add
  // entries to make a new script pass.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    "rootDir": "."
  },
  "include": [
    // The Express `Request.user` augmentation. Ambient, so it is not reachable by import and
    // must be named or every `req.user` in a transitively-included controller fails.
    "src/types/express.d.ts",
    "scripts/**/*"
  ],
  "exclude": [
    "node_modules",
    "dist",
    // Script TESTS are included, not excluded. They used to be listed here "for the same reason
    // tsconfig.build.json excludes the suites under src/" - and that reason is gone: the backend
    // job now runs `npm run typecheck` over the suites under src/ too. Vitest transpiles without
    // checking types, so an excluded test file is one that nothing checks at all.
    //
    // ── Pre-existing type debt. Shrink this list; never grow it. ──
    // Whole subtrees rather than the 9 individual failing files: an excluded file is still
    // pulled in when an INCLUDED file imports it, and both of these have a clean-looking
    // entry point importing a failing helper (verify-stripe.ts -> verify-stripe/fixtures.ts),
    // so naming the leaves alone does nothing. Every script outside these two directories is
    // covered by default, which is the property that matters.
    "scripts/bg-import/**",
    "scripts/verify-stripe/**",
    // The entry point too: it imports verify-stripe/fixtures.ts, and an excluded file is
    // still checked when an included file imports it, so excluding only the helpers is a
    // no-op. Removing this line is the LAST step of paying that subtree's debt, not the first.
    "scripts/verify-stripe.ts",
    "scripts/verify-assigned-user-staging.ts"
  ]
}
